cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "PseudoForge: a multi-engine pseudopotential generator and validator"
message: >-
  If you use PseudoForge in work you publish, please cite it. Please also cite
  the atomic code that actually produced your pseudopotentials and the library
  whose parameters were used — PseudoForge drives other people's generators and
  reproduces other people's libraries, and those are the scientific
  contributions behind any file it writes. Running `pseudoforge --attribution`
  prints the citations for the engines and libraries a given run touched, and
  every run writes CITATION.txt beside its output.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: "Mohammad H."
    family-names: Amirkhani
    email: mohammadhossienamirkhani@gmail.com
    website: "https://amirkhani.me"
repository-code: "https://github.com/MHAmirkhani/pseudoforge"
url: "https://amirkhani.me"
abstract: >-
  PseudoForge generates and validates pseudopotentials by driving nine
  independent atomic structure codes — Quantum ESPRESSO's ld1, ONCVPSP, the
  Vanderbilt ultrasoft generator, fhi98PP, OPIUM, the Goedecker-Teter-Hutter
  fitting toolchain, AtomPAW, the Atomic Pseudopotential Engine and ADPACK —
  through a single interface, in pure Bash with no Python dependency.

  It reproduces published libraries from those libraries' own published
  parameters rather than approximating them: PSlibrary is reproduced
  byte-identically, and the GTH coefficient tables are reproduced exactly for
  all 322 published element-functional combinations. Where a library's
  parameters are not published, PseudoForge says so and labels its own
  construction as such rather than issuing it under the library's name.

  Validation asks each engine for its own analysis rather than forcing one
  engine's test on all of them, and records which analysis produced each
  verdict. Every run writes a provenance file recording the engine version and
  a fingerprint of the binary that ran, because two builds of the same engine
  version do not produce the same pseudopotential.
keywords:
  - pseudopotential
  - density functional theory
  - electronic structure
  - Quantum ESPRESSO
  - PAW
  - norm-conserving
  - ultrasoft
license: GPL-2.0-or-later
version: 1.3.2
# Provenance of the DOIs below: the ONCVPSP, Vanderbilt, GTH, HGH and both
# SSSP entries are the ones already recorded and checked in lib/metadata.sh.
# The PAW, Troullier-Martins and Rappe entries were taken from the reference
# list supplied by the author, which gave these DOIs explicitly.
references:
  - type: article
    title: "Optimized norm-conserving Vanderbilt pseudopotentials"
    authors:
      - family-names: Hamann
        given-names: "D. R."
    doi: 10.1103/PhysRevB.88.085117
    notes: "ONCVPSP, the engine behind the SG15 and PseudoDojo norm-conserving tables."
  - type: article
    title: "Soft self-consistent pseudopotentials in a generalized eigenvalue formalism"
    authors:
      - family-names: Vanderbilt
        given-names: "D."
    doi: 10.1103/PhysRevB.41.7892
    notes: "The ultrasoft formalism and the generator behind the GBRV library."
  - type: article
    title: "Separable dual-space Gaussian pseudopotentials"
    authors:
      - family-names: Goedecker
        given-names: "S."
      - family-names: Teter
        given-names: "M."
      - family-names: Hutter
        given-names: "J."
    doi: 10.1103/PhysRevB.54.1703
    notes: "GTH."
  - type: article
    title: "Relativistic separable dual-space Gaussian pseudopotentials from H to Rn"
    authors:
      - family-names: Hartwigsen
        given-names: "C."
      - family-names: Goedecker
        given-names: "S."
      - family-names: Hutter
        given-names: "J."
    doi: 10.1103/PhysRevB.58.3641
    notes: "HGH, the relativistic extension of GTH."
  - type: article
    title: "Projector augmented-wave method"
    authors:
      - family-names: "Blöchl"
        given-names: "P. E."
    doi: 10.1103/PhysRevB.50.17953
    notes: "The PAW method, implemented by AtomPAW and by ld1."
  - type: article
    title: "Efficient pseudopotentials for plane-wave calculations"
    authors:
      - family-names: Troullier
        given-names: "N."
      - family-names: Martins
        given-names: "J. L."
    doi: 10.1103/PhysRevB.43.1993
    notes: "The Troullier-Martins scheme, used by several engines here."
  - type: article
    title: "Optimized pseudopotentials"
    authors:
      - family-names: Rappe
        given-names: "A. M."
      - family-names: Rabe
        given-names: "K. M."
      - family-names: Kaxiras
        given-names: "E."
      - family-names: Joannopoulos
        given-names: "J. D."
    doi: 10.1103/PhysRevB.41.1227
    notes: "The optimized scheme behind OPIUM."
  - type: article
    title: >-
      Precision and efficiency in solid-state pseudopotential calculations
    authors:
      - family-names: Prandini
        given-names: "G."
      - family-names: Marrazzo
        given-names: "A."
      - family-names: Castelli
        given-names: "I. E."
      - family-names: Mounet
        given-names: "N."
      - family-names: Marzari
        given-names: "N."
    doi: 10.1038/s41524-018-0127-2
    notes: "SSSP, the verification protocol and selection PseudoForge resolves."
  - type: data
    title: >-
      A Standard Solid State Pseudopotentials (SSSP) library optimized for
      precision and efficiency
    doi: 10.24435/materialscloud:f3-ym
    notes: "The SSSP selection tables bundled with PseudoForge (version 1.3.0)."
